Yes—with one important distinction.

If you use “hallucination” broadly to mean generating a coherent representation that is not fully constrained by reality, then humans absolutely hallucinate all the time. Usually we just give the phenomena different names: confabulation, false memory, expectation, illusion, pareidolia, dreaming, filling in the blind spot, reconstructive memory, motivated reasoning, prediction, imagination.

The interesting point is that this may not be a defect accidentally bolted onto intelligence. It may be partly the price of having a generative mind at all.

A useful abstraction is:

incomplete sensory/input data + prior model + prediction → constructed experience

That describes a surprising amount of human cognition. Your brain does not passively record the world like a camera. It infers what is probably out there, continuously filling gaps and correcting predictions against incoming evidence.

An LLM does something structurally analogous:

incomplete context + learned model + prediction → constructed continuation

When the external constraint becomes weak relative to the model’s priors, either system can produce something internally coherent but externally wrong.

So there are really three cases:

1. Human clinical hallucination: perception generated without the corresponding external stimulus.
2. Ordinary human constructive cognition: the brain predicts/fills in information, sometimes incorrectly.
3. AI “hallucination”: generated assertions that fit the model’s learned statistical world but aren’t adequately grounded in actual facts.

And there’s a deeper consequence to your observation: perfectly eliminating generativity would probably destroy some of the capabilities we actually want. Imagination, counterfactual reasoning, creativity, analogy, planning, hypothesis generation, dreaming up possibilities—all require producing representations that are not simply copies of current input.

So the engineering problem isn’t necessarily:

“Make the system never hallucinate.”

It’s more like:

“Make it know when it is imagining versus remembering versus perceiving versus verifying.”

Humans aren’t particularly good at that either. We can remember something vividly that never happened, mistake an inference for something we actually saw, or become more certain of a story every time we retell it.

In Pantheon terms, I’d separate generation from grounding rather than treating generation itself as pathological:

perceive → predict → generate → reality-check → label provenance → act

The dangerous failure is not necessarily creating the unreal. It’s losing the provenance tag that says where the representation came from.

A dream that knows it’s a dream is imagination.

A hypothesis that knows it’s a hypothesis is reasoning.

A fictional possibility that knows it’s fictional is creativity.

A fabrication presented as observed fact is hallucination.

So your intuition points toward something fairly deep: hallucination may be the shadow cast by generative intelligence. The higher-order capability is learning to distinguish the shadow from the object.